# SQL joins cheat sheet

```sql
-- inner: only matching pairs
SELECT s.name, e.grade FROM student s JOIN enrolment e ON e.student_id = s.id;

-- left: every student, grade NULL if not enrolled
SELECT s.name, e.grade FROM student s LEFT JOIN enrolment e ON e.student_id = s.id;

-- anti-join: students with no enrolment
SELECT s.name FROM student s
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM enrolment e WHERE e.student_id = s.id);

-- self join: pairs of students in the same section
SELECT a.name, b.name FROM student a JOIN student b
  ON a.section = b.section AND a.id < b.id;
```

- `NOT IN` with a subquery that can return NULL returns nothing; prefer `NOT EXISTS`.
- `USING (col)` merges the join column into one output column.
- Filters on the right table of a LEFT JOIN belong in the ON clause, otherwise the WHERE turns it into an inner join.
- `LATERAL` lets a subquery refer to columns of earlier FROM items (top-3 per group).
